decode the modern icns icon types

MEASURED with the dts ImageIO icns case, which walks ic04 through ic14 and checks each decoded
image. Before: the source answered ZERO images for every one of the ten and the case died on its
first assertion. After: all ten decode, ic04 and ic05 pass every check the case makes, and the run
reaches ic07.

THE READER KNEW ONLY THE CLASSIC TYPES. Its switch covers twenty two OSTypes, every one of them a
raw sample format from the classic era: ICON ICN# icm# icm4 icm8 ics# ics4 ics8 is32 s8mk icl4 icl8
il32 l8mk ich# ich4 ich8 ih32 h8mk it32 t8mk, plus ic08 and ic09 recorded with bitsPerPixel 0,
which the decode below never handles. Eight of the ten types the case covers were absent entirely.
Every icon made in the last fifteen years is one of these, so a modern .icns produced nothing at
all, and that is how every Mac application ships its icon.

DISPATCH IS ON THE PAYLOAD MAGIC, not on the type code, because the encoding is a property of the
chunk: the same type can appear either way. Measured across the ten fixtures, ic04 and ic05 hold
RLE compressed ARGB and the other eight hold a whole PNG. Every file also carries an info chunk of
310 bytes holding a bplist, which matches nothing and is skipped like any other unknown type.

A PNG PAYLOAD IS HANDED TO WHICHEVER SOURCE RECOGNISES IT rather than decoded here.
O2ImageSource sniffs the data and picks the subclass, so the PNG reader that already exists does
the work.

THE ARGB PATH IS NOT PREMULTIPLIED, unlike the classic path in the same file. An ARGB chunk stores
straight alpha and macOS reports kCGImageAlphaLast for these icons. That was wrong in the first
version and the case caught it.

WHAT STILL DIFFERS, and it is not ICNS: the eight PNG backed types come back as
kO2ImageAlphaPremultipliedLast because our PNG source premultiplies, where macOS reports
kCGImageAlphaLast. Changing that touches every PNG in every application, so it belongs in its own
task rather than here. The case now fails there instead of on the very first count.

CIDER_TRACE_ICNS names each chunk and how it was read, which is what separated "ic04 failed" from
"ic04 and ic05 passed and ic07 failed" when both produce an identical assertion message.

